  "worked_solution": "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{3t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032} & ={u}^{\u2032}{e}^{3t}+3u{e}^{3t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{\u2033} & ={u}^{\u2033}{e}^{3t}+6{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{\u2033}{e}^{3t}+6{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t})-6({u}^{\u2032}{e}^{3t}+3u{e}^{3t})+9(u{e}^{3t}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{\u2033}{e}^{3t}+6{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t}-6{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{3t}-18u{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{\u2033}{e}^{3t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2033}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{\u2032}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{\u2032}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{\u2032},$ we have:\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2032}={c}_{1}\\,\u27f9\\,u={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}\n$$\n\nThus, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{3t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={e}^{3t}({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={e}^{3t}\\cdot t=t{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{6t}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3t}+Bt{e}^{3t}.\n$$",
